## Problem
Even when trip analytics is enabled, trips may be silently lost if the H2 database fails to initialize (lock error, permission issue, missing directory, etc.).

## Root Cause
TripDatabase.java:50-106 init() method: after 3 failed attempts, logs an error and breaks out of the loop WITHOUT setting isInitialized = true. The database object is not null (it exists), but isAvailable() returns false.

TripAnalyticsManager.java:534 handleTripEnded() only checks:
  if (database != null) {
    long dbId = database.insertTrip(trip);
  }

This check passes (database != null is true), but insertTrip() internally checks isInitialized and returns -1 or silently fails. The trip is recorded to telemetry files on disk but never persisted to the database, so the web UI sees nothing.

Additionally: when dbId <= 0 (insert failed), no error is logged at TripAnalyticsManager.java:537 — the code just falls through to the next section silently.

## Fix Required

### Part 1 — Add loud logging when trip is discarded (TripAnalyticsManager.java)
At line 534, change:
  if (database != null) {
      long dbId = database.insertTrip(trip);
      if (dbId > 0) {
          // rename telemetry file ...

To also log when database is unavailable OR when insert fails:
  if (database == null || !database.isAvailable()) {
      logger.error('CRITICAL: Trip database unavailable — trip NOT saved! distance=' + trip.distanceKm + 'km');
  } else {
      long dbId = database.insertTrip(trip);
      if (dbId <= 0) {
          logger.error('CRITICAL: database.insertTrip returned ' + dbId + ' — trip NOT saved!');
      } else {
          // existing rename + rollup logic
      }
  }

### Part 2 — Add loud logging in TripDatabase.init() when all retries fail
After line 102 (the break in the else branch), add:
  logger.error('CRITICAL: Trip database failed to initialize after ' + maxRetries + ' attempts — no trips will be saved');

## Relevant files
- app/src/main/java/com/loabletech/bladewatch/trips/TripDatabase.java:50-106 (init method)
- app/src/main/java/com/loabletech/bladewatch/trips/TripAnalyticsManager.java:530-580 (handleTripEnded)

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] When H2 init fails, logcat shows a CRITICAL error message (not just the original exception)
- [ ] When a trip is discarded due to unavailable database, logcat shows a CRITICAL error
- [ ] When insertTrip returns <= 0, logcat shows a CRITICAL error
- [ ] Build compiles without errors (./gradlew assembleDebug passes)

Do not make mistakes. Read TripDatabase.java and TripAnalyticsManager.java fully before editing. Do not change any logic — only add logging. Verify ./gradlew assembleDebug passes before closing.

## Problem
SentryDaemon takes ~1000ms to start. enableWifi() at lines 134 and 141 of SentryDaemon.java accounts for ~845ms of that. It calls execShell(CMD_WIFI_ENABLE()) and execShell(CMD_WIFI_ENABLE_ALT()) sequentially with blocking waitFor() — the BYD Wi-Fi HAL takes 400-700ms per call to respond.

Neither return value of execShell() is used in enableWifi(). The daemon does not check WiFi state after this call. WiFi enabling can happen asynchronously.

## Files
- app/src/main/java/com/loabletech/bladewatch/daemon/SentryDaemon.java

## Exact Change Required

### Change enableWifi() method (lines 317-321)
Current:
  private static void enableWifi() {
      log('Enabling WiFi...');
      execShell(CMD_WIFI_ENABLE());
      execShell(CMD_WIFI_ENABLE_ALT());
      log('WiFi enabled');
  }

Replace with:
  private static void enableWifi() {
      log('Enabling WiFi (async)...');
      new Thread(() -> {
          execShell(CMD_WIFI_ENABLE());
          execShell(CMD_WIFI_ENABLE_ALT());
          log('WiFi enable commands completed');
      }, 'WifiEnable').start();
  }

That is the complete change. The thread calls the existing execShell() helper which already handles waitFor() and stream draining — so file descriptors are properly reaped and no zombie processes accumulate.

### What NOT to change
- Do NOT modify execShell() itself — it is used by many other callers that need its blocking/return-value behaviour
- Do NOT change any other method
- enableWifi() is called from two sites (lines 134 and 141) — both benefit automatically since the method itself changes

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Build compiles with ./gradlew compileDebugJavaWithJavac
- [ ] enableWifi() spawns a background thread named 'WifiEnable'
- [ ] execShell() is unchanged
- [ ] Both call sites (lines 134 and 141) benefit without any additional changes

Do not make mistakes. Read SentryDaemon.java fully before editing. Verify the build compiles. If anything is ambiguous, stop and ask rather than guessing.

## Problem
CameraDaemon takes 8.2s to start. initSurveillance() accounts for 6072ms (74%) of that. The bottleneck is YoloDetector.init() inside SurveillanceEngineGpu.init() — TFLite GPU delegate must compile OpenCL/Vulkan kernels on every cold start, which blocks the entire daemon startup.

MotionPipelineV2 (pixel-diff motion detection) is fully independent of YOLO and can start immediately. YOLO only classifies motion events after MotionPipelineV2 detects them — deferring YOLO does not break motion recording.

## Files
- app/src/main/java/com/loabletech/bladewatch/surveillance/SurveillanceEngineGpu.java

## Exact Changes Required

### Fix 1: Make useObjectDetection volatile (line 123)
Current:
  private boolean useObjectDetection = false;
Change to:
  private volatile boolean useObjectDetection = false;
Reason: useObjectDetection is read on the camera frame thread and will be written from a background thread after this change. Without volatile, the frame thread can see a stale false indefinitely (no memory barrier).

### Fix 2: Fix baseline seeding guard (line 821)
Current condition:
  if (!baselineSeeded && frameCount == 30 && useObjectDetection && yoloDetector != null)
Change to:
  if (!baselineSeeded && frameCount >= 30 && useObjectDetection && yoloDetector != null)
Reason: if YOLO loads after frame 30, the baseline is never seeded for the entire session with the == check, disabling static-object filtering and causing false-positive recordings for the whole session.

### Fix 3: Move YoloDetector.init() to a background thread in init()
Lines 414-460 contain two code paths that call yoloDetector.init() (one with context, one with assetContext). Both must be deferred identically.

For the context path (lines 414-429), replace the synchronous block with:
  Thread yoloInitThread = new Thread(() -> {
      try {
          YoloDetector detector = new YoloDetector(context);
          boolean loaded = detector.init();
          if (loaded) {
              yoloDetector = detector;       // volatile write — publishes state
              useObjectDetection = true;     // written BEFORE yoloDetector so frame thread sees consistent pair
          } else {
              logger.warn('YOLO model load failed (deferred) - object detection disabled');
          }
      } catch (Exception e) {
          logger.warn('Deferred YOLO init error: ' + e.getMessage());
      }
  }, 'YoloInit');
  yoloInitThread.setDaemon(true);
  yoloInitThread.start();

Apply identical pattern for the assetContext path (lines 437-452).

IMPORTANT: Write useObjectDetection = true BEFORE writing yoloDetector (volatile), so the volatile write on yoloDetector acts as the memory barrier publishing both writes to the frame thread. The frame thread already snapshots yoloDetector into a local variable before use (line 829 pattern) — maintain this discipline.

IMPORTANT: Do NOT move pipelineV2 init (lines 467-475) or buffer allocations to a background thread. Only the YoloDetector blocks.

### What NOT to change
- pipelineV2 init (lines 467-475) stays synchronous
- ByteBuffer allocations stay synchronous
- baselineSeeded field is already volatile (line 310) — no change needed
- yoloDetector field is already volatile (line 126) — no change needed

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Build compiles with ./gradlew compileDebugJavaWithJavac
- [ ] useObjectDetection is declared volatile at line 123
- [ ] frameCount condition at line 821 uses >= not ==
- [ ] YoloDetector.init() runs on a background thread named 'YoloInit'
- [ ] pipelineV2 init remains synchronous
- [ ] No other surveillance logic is moved off the init thread

Do not make mistakes. Read SurveillanceEngineGpu.java fully before editing. Verify the build compiles. If anything is ambiguous, stop and ask rather than guessing.

