## Problem
StorageManager constructor (called at CameraDaemon startup) runs discoverSdCard() synchronously at line 194, which executes 'sm list-volumes all' via process.waitFor() plus shell touch/rm write probes. This costs ~150-200ms on every cold start even though the SD card UUID almost never changes.

## Files
- app/src/main/java/com/loabletech/bladewatch/storage/StorageManager.java

## Exact Changes Required

### Cache file path constant (add near top of class, after existing constants around line 109)
  private static final String SD_CARD_CACHE_PATH = '/data/local/tmp/bladewatch_sdcard_path';

### Add tryLoadSdCardFromCache() method (add as a private method near discoverSdCard())
  private boolean tryLoadSdCardFromCache() {
      try {
          File cacheFile = new File(SD_CARD_CACHE_PATH);
          if (!cacheFile.exists()) return false;
          String cachedPath = new java.util.Scanner(cacheFile).useDelimiter('\A').next().trim();
          if (cachedPath.isEmpty()) return false;
          // Validate the cached path is still writable before trusting it
          if (!shellCanWrite(cachedPath)) {
              Log.d(TAG, 'Cached SD path not writable, falling back to full discovery: ' + cachedPath);
              cacheFile.delete();
              return false;
          }
          sdCardPath = cachedPath;
          sdCardAvailable = true;
          Log.i(TAG, 'SD card path loaded from cache: ' + cachedPath);
          return true;
      } catch (Exception e) {
          Log.w(TAG, 'SD card cache read failed: ' + e.getMessage());
          return false;
      }
  }

### Add writeSdCardCache() method (add near tryLoadSdCardFromCache())
  private void writeSdCardCache(String path) {
      try {
          // Atomic write: write to temp file then rename to avoid torn reads on crash
          File tmpFile = new File(SD_CARD_CACHE_PATH + '.tmp');
          java.io.FileWriter fw = new java.io.FileWriter(tmpFile, false);
          fw.write(path);
          fw.close();
          tmpFile.renameTo(new File(SD_CARD_CACHE_PATH));
      } catch (Exception e) {
          Log.w(TAG, 'SD card cache write failed: ' + e.getMessage());
      }
  }

### Modify the constructor call site (line 194): replace discoverSdCard() with cache-first logic
Current (line 194):
  discoverSdCard();

Replace with:
  if (!tryLoadSdCardFromCache()) {
      discoverSdCard();
  }

### Write cache after successful full discovery: inside discoverSdCard() (lines 523-524), after setting sdCardPath and sdCardAvailable=true
Current (lines 523-524):
  sdCardPath = candidate;
  sdCardAvailable = true;

Add after line 524:
  writeSdCardCache(candidate);

Also add after lines 294-295 (the sm list-volumes success path) and lines 344-346 (the /storage scan path) if they exist — check the file for all places where sdCardAvailable = true is set inside discoverSdCard().

### Scope: cache is ONLY used at the constructor call site (line 194)
The watchdog/remount code at line 367 calls discoverSdCard() directly — do NOT change that call. The watchdog's entire job is detecting when the card comes back, which requires a real probe.

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Build compiles with ./gradlew compileDebugJavaWithJavac
- [ ] On first boot (no cache file), discoverSdCard() runs normally
- [ ] On subsequent boots, tryLoadSdCardFromCache() validates path with shellCanWrite() before setting sdCardAvailable=true
- [ ] If shellCanWrite() fails on cached path, falls back to full discoverSdCard()
- [ ] writeSdCardCache() uses atomic temp+rename write
- [ ] Watchdog call at line 367 is unchanged (still calls discoverSdCard() directly)
- [ ] All places inside discoverSdCard() that set sdCardAvailable=true also call writeSdCardCache()

Do not make mistakes. Read StorageManager.java fully before editing. Verify the build compiles. If anything is ambiguous, stop and ask rather than guessing.

## Problem

When the user taps Lock, Unlock, Trunk Open, Flash Lights, or Find Car, there is no visual feedback while the command is in-flight. The 600ms debounce prevents immediate double-taps but the button is re-enabled after 600ms while the HTTP request (8s timeout) is still running. Users may tap repeatedly thinking nothing happened, sending duplicate commands to the vehicle.

**File:** app/src/main/java/com/loabletech/bladewatch/ui/fragment/vehicle/VehicleController.kt  
**Lines 663-669:** doVehicleAction() — no in-progress UI state  
**Lines 710-716:** buildActionButton() — no disabled state support

## Fix

One lightweight approach: disable ALL action buttons while a command is in-flight using an inFlight AtomicBoolean:
```kotlin
private val inFlight = AtomicBoolean(false)

private fun doVehicleAction(key: String, action: () -> Boolean) {
    if (!debounce(key)) return
    if (!inFlight.compareAndSet(false, true)) return  // already running
    Thread({
        val ok = action()
        inFlight.set(false)
        root.post {
            if (!ok) toast("Action failed. Check vehicle connection.")
            renderTabContent()  // re-enable buttons
        }
    }, "VehicleAction-$key").apply { isDaemon = true; start() }
    renderTabContent()  // re-render with visual hint
}
```

In buildActionButton(), check inFlight.get() and render the button as dimmed (alpha 0.5) when in-flight.

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Tapping a vehicle command button while another command is in-flight does nothing (no duplicate request)
- [ ] Button visually indicates in-progress state (dimmed, color change, or Toast)
- [ ] After success or failure, buttons return to normal
- [ ] Build passes (./gradlew assembleDebug)

Do not make mistakes. Read VehicleController.kt fully before editing.
